    {
      "id": "kuxssef",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "FUNDAMENTAL DUTIES"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Fundamental Duties were incorporated into Part IV-A of the Constitution under Article 51A based on the Swaran Singh Committee recommendations.",
        "Originally 10 duties were added by the 42nd Constitutional Amendment Act (1976), and the 11th duty was added by the 86th Amendment Act (2002).",
        "Fundamental Duties are non-justiciable in nature and cannot be directly enforced by courts without specific parliamentary legislation.",
        "They serve as a reminder to citizens of their obligations toward the nation and apply exclusively to citizens of India.",
        "Statutes like the Prevention of Insults to National Honour Act and Wildlife Protection Act help enforce specific fundamental duties legally."
      ],
      "id": "ezaqqnf"
    },
